1. What is Mockito, and why is it used with JUnit?

Answer: Mockito is a mocking framework that creates test doubles (mocks/spies) to isolate dependencies in unit tests. It helps:
Replace real dependencies with controlled mocks.
Verify interactions (e.g., verify(mock).call()).
Stub method calls (e.g., when(mock.method()).thenReturn(value)).

2. Explain the difference between @Mock and @InjectMocks.

@Mock: Creates a mock object (e.g., @Mock UserRepository userRepo).
@InjectMocks: Injects mocks into the class under test (e.g., @InjectMocks UserService userService).

@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)
class UserServiceTest {
    @Mock UserRepository userRepo;
    @InjectMocks UserService userService;
}

3. How do you stub a method to return a specific value?
when(mockUserRepo.findById(1L)).thenReturn(Optional.of(new User(1L, "Alice")));

4. How do you stub a method to throw an exception?
when(mockUserRepo.save(any())).thenThrow(new RuntimeException("DB error"));

5. What’s the difference between doReturn() and thenReturn()?
thenReturn: Used for standard stubbing.
doReturn: Used when spying real objects to avoid actual method execution.
doReturn("fake").when(spy).realMethod(); // Skips realMethod()

6. How do you verify a method was called exactly once?
